## Data

Our data series consists of monthly unemployment rates for the whole United States, as well as the states of Oregon, California, Texas, and New York. It came directly from FRED, or the Federal Reserve Economic Data database, starting from January 1st, 1976 to October, 2025.

```{r}
library(tidyverse)
library(knitr)

unemp_raw <- read_rds("data/unemp_raw.rds")

head(unemp_raw)
```

## Wrangling

```{r}
library(knitr)
```

Note: Installed the knitr package to use the kable() function. Used digits = 2 for rounding precision and presentation.

```{r}
unemp <- unemp_raw |>
  rename(unemployment_rate = price) |>
  mutate(
    region = case_when(
      symbol == "UNRATE" ~ "United States",
      symbol == "ORUR" ~ "Oregon",
      symbol == "CAUR" ~ "California",
      symbol == "TXUR" ~ "Texas",
      symbol == "NYUR" ~ "New York"
    )
  ) |>
  arrange(region, date) |>
  group_by(region) |>
  mutate(
    unemployment_rate = replace(
      unemployment_rate,
      date == as.Date("2025-10-01"),
      unemployment_rate[date == as.Date("2025-09-01")]
    ),
    unemployment_rate_change =
      unemployment_rate - lag(unemployment_rate)
  ) |>
  ungroup()

unemp_summary <- unemp |>
  group_by(region) |>
  summarize(
    mean_rate = mean(unemployment_rate, na.rm = TRUE),
    min_rate = min(unemployment_rate, na.rm = TRUE),
    max_rate = max(unemployment_rate, na.rm = TRUE)
  )

unemp_recent <- unemp |>
  filter(date >= as.Date("2015-01-01"))

unemp_recent_summary <- unemp_recent |>
  group_by(region) |>
  summarize(
    mean_rate = mean(unemployment_rate, na.rm = TRUE),
    min_rate = min(unemployment_rate, na.rm = TRUE),
    max_rate = max(unemployment_rate, na.rm = TRUE)
  )
```

Note: Did this to avoid the mentioned FRED data issues as advised in announcement.

### Table 1: Full Unemployment Data

Table 1 gives us the mean, minimum, and maximum unemployment rates for each region (US, Oregon, California, Texas, and New York) across the full data period, meaning from January 1976 to October 2025.

```{r}
kable(unemp_summary, digits = 2)
```

### Table 2: Unemployment Date January 2015 and Onward

Table 2 was filtered to give us the mean, minimum, and maximum unemployment rates for each region (US, Oregon, California, Texas, and New York) from January 2015 onward.

```{r}
kable(unemp_recent_summary, digits = 2)
```

### Table 3: Largest Monthly Increases in Unemployment

Table 3 excluded data observations in 2020, due to the pandemic having an impact on that data otherwise unforseen. It additionally shows the largest month to month increases in unemployment rate for each region (US, Oregon, California, Texas, and New York).

```{r}
unemp_top_increases_no_covid <- unemp |>
  filter(format(date, "%Y") != "2020") |>
  slice_max(
    order_by = unemployment_rate_change,
    n = 5,
    with_ties = TRUE
  )

kable(unemp_top_increases_no_covid, digits = 2)
```

### Table 4: Largest Monthly Decreases in Unemployment

Table 4 also excluded data observations in 2020, due to the pandemic having an impact on that data otherwise unforseen. However, it instead shows the largest month to month *decreases* in unemployment rate for each region (US, Oregon, California, Texas, and New York).

```{r}
unemp_top_decreases_no_covid <- unemp |>
  filter(format(date, "%Y") != "2020") |>
  slice_min(
    order_by = unemployment_rate_change,
    n = 5,
    with_ties = TRUE
  )

kable(unemp_top_decreases_no_covid, digits = 2)
```

## Visualization

### Plot 1: Unemployment Rates Overtime (Full Data)

Plot 1 compares the monthly unemployment rates across each region (US, Oregon, California, Texas, and New York) across the entire data period, meaning from January 1976 to October 2025. Different colors represent a different region, e.g. red for California, showing trends in the unemployment rates overtime.

```{r}
ggplot(unemp, aes(x = date, y = unemployment_rate, color = region)) +
  geom_line() +
  labs(
    title = "Unemployment Rates by Region Overtime",
    x = "Date",
    y = "Unemployment Rate (Percent)",
    color = "Region"
  ) +
  theme_minimal()
```

### Plot 2: Unemployment Rates by Region

Plot 2 still compares the monthly unemployment rates across each region (US, Oregon, California, Texas, and New York) across the entire data period, meaning from January 1976 to October 2025. However, it uses different panels to illustrate each region's trends, rather than an overlapping view seen in Plot 1.

```{r}
ggplot(unemp, aes(x = date, y = unemployment_rate, color = region)) +
  geom_line() +
  facet_wrap(~ region) +
  labs(
    title = "Unemployment Rates by Region Overtime",
    x = "Date",
    y = "Unemployment Rate (Percent)"
  ) +
  theme_minimal()
```

### Plot 3: The Mean Unemployment Rates by Region

Plot 3 still compares the monthly unemployment rates across each region (US, Oregon, California, Texas, and New York) across the entire data period, meaning from January 1976 to October 2025. However, it utilizes a bar chart instead of a line graph, ordered by the mean values of each region's unemployment rates.

```{r}
ggplot(
  unemp_summary,
  aes(
    x = fct_reorder(region, mean_rate),
    y = mean_rate,
    fill = region
  )
) +
  geom_col() +
  labs(
    title = "Mean Unemployment Rates by Region",
    x = "Region",
    y = "Mean Unemployment Rate (Percent)"
  ) +
  theme_minimal()
```

### Plot 4: Great Recession vs Covid Pandemic

Plot 4 compares the monthly unemployment rates across each region (US, Oregon, California, Texas, and New York) during the Great Recession defined as between 2007-2010, and the Covid Pandemic, defined as 2019-2022. The reason I choose to explore these two periods was because I was curious how they compare in unemployment rates given their differing elements and origins. A financial crisis vs global pandemic are differing shocks but both have devastating consequences.

```{r}
unemp_recessions <- unemp |>
  filter(
    (date >= as.Date("2007-01-01") &
       date <= as.Date("2010-12-01")) |
      (date >= as.Date("2019-01-01") &
         date <= as.Date("2022-12-01"))
  ) |>
  mutate(
    period = if_else(
      date < as.Date("2011-01-01"),
      "Great Recession (2007-2010)",
      "COVID-19 (2019-2022)"
    )
  )

ggplot(
  unemp_recessions,
  aes(x = date, y = unemployment_rate, color = region)
) +
  geom_line() +
  facet_wrap(~ period) +
  labs(
    title = "Unemployment During the Great Recession and COVID-19",
    x = "Date",
    y = "Unemployment Rate (Percent)",
    color = "Region"
  ) +
  theme_minimal()
```

